An event stream reconnects when the connection ends, fails or stays silent for the idle timeout: it asks again with fresh headers and `Last-Event-ID`, waits the server's `retry` value or `WithReconnectDelay` (1 second, doubling to 30 seconds with jitter), and raises the last failure after `WithMaxReconnects` failures in a row without an event (5; 0 for a request with a body; `-1` is unlimited). It never reconnects after a 204, a status other than 408, 429 and 5xx, a response that is not `text/event-stream`, a cancellation or `client.Close()`. An event named `error` raises `ApiError` with the response status and the data as its body, and a comment line such as a heartbeat counts as activity for the idle timeout. The generator option `sseTerminator` names a data value (such as `[DONE]`) that ends the stream and is not delivered. The call options `WithIdleTimeout` and `WithLastEventID` also apply.

### Pagination

An operation that returns a list one page at a time has a `<operation>Pages` method next to its plain method. It takes the same parameters in the same order and returns a `*Paginator[T, C]`, where `T` is the item type and `C` is the type of the cursor parameter. The method sends no request: the first request goes out when you first read an item or a page, and each next request goes out only when you ask for more, so stopping early sends nothing further and leaves nothing to close. Range over `Items()` for the items, or over `Pages()` for `*Page[T, C]` values with `Items`, `NextCursor` and `Result`, an `ApiResult` that holds the status, the headers, the raw body and the decoded body of the page, so the status and headers stay reachable. Iterating again starts again from the starting position.
{{#hasPagedOperation}}

```go file
import (
	"context"
	"fmt"

	"{{packageName}}/pkg"
{{#pagedOperation}}
{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}
{{#isOptions}}
	opts "{{packageName}}/pkg/options"
{{/isOptions}}
{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}
)

func listEverything(ctx context.Context, client *{{packageName}}.{{clientClassName}}{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}, {{#isPath}}{{paramName}} {{{dataType}}}{{/isPath}}{{#isOptions}}options *opts.{{{dataType}}}{{/isOptions}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}) error {
	list := client.{{#pagedGroup}}{{baseName}}{{/pagedGroup}}.{{vendorExtensions.op.pagesMethodName}}(ctx{{#vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}}, {{paramName}}{{/vendorExtensions.op.signatureArgs}})
	for item, err := range list.Items() {
		if err != nil {
			return err
		}
		fmt.Println(item)
	}
	for page, err := range list.Pages() {
		if err != nil {
			return err
		}
		fmt.Println(len(page.Items), "items, status", page.Result.StatusCode)
	}
	return nil
}
```
{{/pagedOperation}}
{{/hasPagedOperation}}

The cursor you pass to the method is the starting position, and the next cursor of a page resumes the list: pass it as the starting cursor of a new call. The page size and every other parameter, option and header are repeated unchanged on each page request, and each page is an ordinary call with its own retries, timeout, redirects, authentication and redaction. An error on a later page is yielded from the read that needed it, after the items of the earlier pages were delivered, and ends the iteration; the SDK never skips a page. A list ends when the next cursor is absent, `nil` or empty, and an empty page with a next cursor continues. A page that returns the cursor that was just sent yields a `PaginationError`, a direct child of `{{errorPrefix}}Error`, after its items were delivered, instead of looping. There is no total limit and no collect-all: break out of the loop.

How each style ends and resumes: an id list passes the `last_id` or `next_cursor` of each page as the starting cursor of the next request and ends when a declared `has_more` is false. An offset list starts at the offset you pass, sends the offset past the items it received on each next request, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A page-number list sends the page you pass, or none, and then asks for the page after each one, and ends on an empty page or when a declared `has_more` is false. A next URL list takes the URL in the body of each page, follows it only within the origin of the client, and ends when the property is absent, `null` or empty. A cursor, offset or page that does not advance raises `PaginationError` as a token cursor does.

A list that is followed by a `Link` header (`rel="next"`, RFC 8288) takes the next URL from the header of each response. A next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError` and nothing, no credentials either, is sent to it; such a method has no starting cursor, so it cannot resume.

A list whose page body carries the next URL in a property (a next URL list) is followed in the same way as a `Link` list: the URL is taken from the body of each response, a next URL that leaves the scheme, host or port of the client is refused with `PaginationError`, nothing is sent to it, and such a method cannot resume from a saved position.

The generator finds a paged operation by its names and shape. The operation must be a GET with one JSON 2xx response, and it must match exactly one of these styles. A token cursor is one query parameter among `cursor`, `after`, `page_token`, `next_token`, `continuation_token` and `marker`, paired with a next-cursor property among `next_cursor`, `next_page_token`, `next_token`, `next_marker`, `next_continuation_token`, `continuation_token` and `next` of the same type. An id cursor is one query parameter among `starting_after`, `after_id` and `after`, paired with a boolean `has_more` and a string `last_id` or `next_cursor` property. A `Link` list has a `Link` header declared on the response. An offset list has an `offset` query parameter with a `limit`, and a page-number list has a `page` query parameter with one of `per_page`, `page_size`, `size` or `limit`. A next URL list has no cursor parameter and a string property with `format: uri` or `uri-reference`, or one named `next_url`, `next_uri`, `nextlink` or `next_link`, that is optional or nullable. An operation that matches more than one style is ambiguous, and one that is streamed is never paged; either keeps only its plain method, and the generation log names it and says why. The `x-fern-pagination` and `x-speakeasy-pagination` (cursor type) extensions are read as hints. The generator option `paginationOverrides` (a JSON object that maps an operation id to `none` or to `{style, requestParam, responseProperty, itemsProperty, hasMoreProperty}`, where the style is `token`, `link`, `id`, `offset`, `page` or `body`) wins over a hint and over inference, and `pagination=off` switches the feature off.

### Custom HTTP client

`TransportOptionsBuilder.HttpClient(doer)` accepts anything with a
`Do(*http.Request) (*http.Response, error)` method (an `*http.Client`
qualifies) and `TransportOptionsBuilder.RoundTripper(rt)` wraps your
`http.RoundTripper`. The SDK keeps its defaults on top (user agent, headers,
timeout and, around a RoundTripper or an `*http.Client`'s transport, its
redirect policy) and never closes what you supply. Combining a supplied
transport with `Proxy`, `CACertPath` or `VerifySsl(false)` is rejected by
`Build()` with `ErrTransportConflict`: configure those on your own transport.

### Testing your code

Every API group has an interface (`{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}{{classname}}Interface{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}`) and the client has one (`{{clientClassName}}`, a struct whose group fields are typed as those interfaces), so a test can replace the SDK with a hand-written fake: no mocking library, no network.

```go file
import "{{packageName}}/pkg"

{{#apiInfo}}{{#apis}}{{#-first}}{{#operations}}// Code under test takes the group interface, never the generated struct.
func use(api {{packageName}}.{{classname}}Interface) {}

// The fake embeds the interface, so only the operations you fake are written.
type fake{{classname}} struct {
    {{packageName}}.{{classname}}Interface
}

func example() {
    fake := fake{{classname}}{}
    use(fake)

    client := &{{packageName}}.{{clientClassName}}{ {{baseName}}: fake }
    defer client.Close() // a client of fakes holds no transport, so Close returns nil
    _ = client.{{baseName}}
}{{/operations}}{{/-first}}{{/apis}}{{/apiInfo}}
```

A new operation in the API adds a method to the interface, so a fake that implements the whole interface stops compiling after you regenerate; embed the interface so only the operation you fake is written.

### Credentials

`New{{clientClassName}}WithToken(host, accessToken, transportOptions)` builds a client with a Bearer token. Each credential is resolved once, when the client is built: the argument you pass wins, then the environment variable (`{{envVarPrefix}}_BASE_URL` for the host, `{{envVarPrefix}}_TOKEN` for the token); an empty value counts as unset. A host or token that cannot be resolved returns `ErrMissingCredential` there and then, not on the first request. `New{{clientClassName}}WithTokenProvider(host, provider, transportOptions)` takes a function returning the current token instead of a static one; the function runs for every request and receives the context of the call.

Each security scheme has an authenticator of its own in `pkg/auth` (`pkg/auth/oauth` for OAuth2 and OpenID Connect), and its constructor resolves credentials the same way. A credential you pass wins. An empty string is read once, when the authenticator is built, from the variable of that scheme, and an empty variable counts as unset. A credential found in neither place returns an error that matches `auth.ErrMissingCredential` (and, for a Bearer scheme, `auth.ErrInvalidBearerToken`) and names the variable, so the client fails when it is built and not on the first request. To hold a credential for several security schemes in one client, build it with `New{{clientClassName}}WithCredentials(host, {{packageName}}.Credentials{...}, transportOptions)`: `Credentials` has one optional `Authenticator` field per scheme, named after the scheme. Each operation uses the credential of the scheme its spec requires (the first scheme it accepts, in spec order, that you set), never one of a scheme it does not accept, and an operation declared without security sends none. A call cannot override the scheme. A Bearer scheme also has a `New<Scheme>AuthenticatorWithProvider(host, provider)` constructor, which asks the provider for the token on every request and reads no variable.
